  <h1>What an AI opportunity audit could look like.</h1>
  <p class="page-lede">A worked example of missed follow-ups in a B2B company, the evidence to examine and the decisions that follow.</p>
  <p class="content-meta">By <a href="/about/">Adrian Ching</a></p>
</div></header>
<article class="reading prose">
  <p>This is a fictional scenario to explain the Audit’s output. It contains no client findings, measured savings or promised results. A real Audit starts with your intake and business data; its priorities may look different.</p>

  <h2>The problem to investigate</h2>
  <p>Imagine a B2B company with repeat customers. Enquiries arrive through several channels, salespeople track some conversations in their inboxes, and the founder suspects that promising leads are being missed. The team is considering an AI tool to generate more leads.</p>
  <p>Before choosing that tool, the review needs to establish what happens to the enquiries the company already receives.</p>

  <h2>What evidence would help?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Enquiry and follow-up records, including arrival time, assigned owner and next action.</li>
    <li>Quotes, orders and lost opportunities that can be connected to those enquiries.</li>
    <li>The team’s explanation of how enquiries move between people and systems.</li>
    <li>The current tools, their costs and the work needed to keep records usable.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Missing records limit what can be concluded. A gap in the CRM does not prove that nobody replied. Conversations and source records would need to be checked before treating that gap as a missed follow-up.</p>

  <h2>Three possible priorities, ranked</h2>
  <p>For this example, assume the review finds inconsistent ownership, overdue follow-ups and customer notes spread across systems. The order below reflects dependencies: reminders and summaries become more useful once the team has reliable records and a clear owner.</p>

  <h3>1. Give each enquiry an owner and a next action</h3>
  <p>Start with a shared record in the existing sales system. Agree who receives each enquiry, who takes over when someone is away, and how the next action is recorded. This could resolve part of the problem without an AI purchase.</p>
  <p>Check whether new enquiries have an owner and a next action before moving on. An unclear handoff needs a working agreement before it needs automation.</p>

  <h3>2. Test reminders for overdue follow-ups</h3>
  <p>If the records are reliable but actions still slip, test the existing system’s reminder features with a small group. Compare overdue actions and response times before and during the trial. Check whether reminders help people act or add more alerts they ignore.</p>
  <p>A separate tool would need to justify its cost, maintenance and integration work against that simpler option.</p>

  <h3>3. Consider AI summaries only if reading history is a bottleneck</h3>
  <p>If salespeople spend time reconstructing customer history, an AI-generated summary may be worth a limited trial. Compare summaries against source conversations, check omissions and errors, and keep a person responsible for the response.</p>
  <p>Access permissions and the handling of customer information need review before any live data is connected. If the history is already easy to read, this trial may not be worth doing.</p>

  <h2>How a 90-day roadmap could follow</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Days 1–30:</strong> Check the records, agree enquiry ownership and establish a baseline for follow-up. Resolve gaps that would make the measurements misleading.</li>
    <li><strong>Days 31–60:</strong> Trial reminders with a limited group. Compare results with the baseline and ask the team what changed in their work.</li>
    <li><strong>Days 61–90:</strong> Decide whether to expand, change or stop the reminder trial. Consider a small summary trial only if reviewing customer history remains a material problem.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>These are illustrative planning stages. They do not state the Audit’s delivery turnaround or commit your team to a particular implementation schedule.</p>

  <h2>What could be estimated, and what remains unknown?</h2>
  <p>Handling time and enquiry volume could support an estimate of time saved, after allowing for review and maintenance. A change in response time alone would not establish extra revenue. That would need evidence connecting follow-up to orders, with other changes in the business taken into account.</p>
  <p>The recommendation could be to improve the current process and defer the AI purchase. The Audit’s job is to rank the options and explain the evidence behind that decision.</p>

